<DocsLayout title="Haft v9 FAQ" description="Current-release answers about FPF Query, typed memory, hosts, authority, execution, storage, and migration.">
  <h1>FAQ</h1>

  <h2>What is the current release?</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/m0n0x41d/haft/releases/tag/v9.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">v9.0.0</a>
    is the current release. v8.1.0 is its predecessor; there was no v8.2
    release.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Haft v9?</h2>

  <p>
    Haft is a source-native FPF delivery and reliance-bearing project-memory
    substrate for principal-led engineering work. It exposes one project graph
    through host-agent skills, a CLI, and MCP.
  </p>

  <h2>Is Haft a coding agent?</h2>

  <p>
    No. v8 removed the standalone agent, TUI, and desktop wrappers. v9 also
    removed <code>haft run</code>, <code>haft harness</code>, and the bundled
    Open-Sleigh runtime. Use Haft through an existing coding agent or an
    independently operated runner.
  </p>

  <h2>Do I need to learn all of FPF first?</h2>

  <p>
    No. <code>h-reason</code> can retrieve the pinned FPF source for the current
    concern. The agent still has to check applicability and boundaries;
    retrieval output is not a selected method or work order.
  </p>

  <h2>Does v9 prescribe a workflow?</h2>

  <p>
    No. The 12 skills are independent capabilities. Use the one whose condition
    is current. Explicit WorkPlans and ordered MethodDescriptions remain valid,
    but order is not inferred from the skill catalog.
  </p>

  <h2>Can I use Haft on an existing project?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes. Run <code>haft init</code> for the exact host, restart it, and inspect
    onboarding status. Init installs default project memory automatically and
    may admit only a complete supported singleton profile as
    <code>detector_default</code>. Ambiguous or reviewed bases stay
    operator-mediated.
  </p>

  <h2>What does onboarding ready mean?</h2>

  <p>
    Only that the canonical project profile and structured project memory are
    available. It does not establish specification applicability, health,
    lifecycle state, evidence truth, or release readiness.
  </p>

  <h2>When does Haft write project memory?</h2>

  <p>
    On an explicit operator request to save or bind, or when current work has a
    concrete receiving use such as handoff, replay, audit, automation, delayed
    evidence, or costly reversal. Empty memory and generic future usefulness
    are not enough.
  </p>

  <h2>Where is data stored?</h2>

  <p>
    Runtime structured state lives in local SQLite under
    <code>~/.haft/projects/&lt;project-id&gt;/</code>. Reviewable project carriers
    live under <code>.haft/</code>. The local cross-project decision-summary
    index lives at <code>~/.haft/index.db</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>How do teams share records?</h2>

  <p>
    Commit supported <code>.haft/*.md</code> carriers through git. Teammates run
    <code>haft sync</code> after pulling to import them into local SQLite.
    Markdown is the exchange carrier; it is not the acting runtime object.
  </p>

  <h2>How do I search in v9?</h2>

  <p>
    There is no <code>/h-search</code> skill. Use the query that matches the
    object: <code>haft fpf query|lookup|inspect</code> for FPF source,
    <code>haft graph explore</code> for code, typed-memory
    <code>resolve|neighborhood|recall</code> for one EntityOfConcern, or an exact
    <code>haft_query</code> project-record action.
  </p>

  <h2>Can the agent bind a decision automatically?</h2>

  <p>
    A supported host may route one direct, unambiguous operator request through
    <code>h-decide</code>. The skill token, a recommendation, quoted text, or
    tool payload is not approval. Ambiguity requires a Human Gate Brief and an
    operator answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Can Haft execute commissioned work?</h2>

  <p>
    Haft records runner-neutral WorkCommission and RuntimeRun lifecycle data.
    It does not execute or apply the work. <code>h-commission</code> is
    manual-only because commission creation grants bounded execution authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Does Haft support Cursor and other hosts?</h2>

  <p>
    Claude Code and Codex are stable v9 hosts. Cursor, Grok, Pi, Hermes, Zed,
    Antigravity, OpenCode, Gemini CLI, and Air have explicit experimental or
    legacy adapter flags. Run <code>haft init --help</code> for the current
    installed surface.
  </p>

  <h2>What is haft-embed in v9?</h2>

  <p>
    <code>haft-embed</code> is an optional compatibility sidecar for older
    semantic-recall paths. It is outside the v9 contract and P14 acceptance
    basis. Its presence does not support a v9 dense/hybrid retrieval-quality
    claim.
  </p>

  <h2>How do I know the host loaded v9?</h2>

  <p>
    Restart the host, record <code>haft version</code> and the binary checksum,
    and inspect the actual MCP process and interface. Source code,
    documentation, or a local test run cannot prove the identity of an already
    running host process.
  </p>

  <h2>What happened to quint-code?</h2>

  <p>
    The project was renamed to Haft in v6. Older <code>.quint/</code>,
    <code>~/.quint-code/</code>, and <code>/q-*</code> surfaces belong to the
    historical migration path. See the <a href="/docs/migration">v8.1 to v9.0 migration guide</a>
    and the archived versioned pages for earlier upgrades.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
